---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('privy')!;
const b = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-mailchimp';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/privy-vs-mailchimp');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/privy-vs-mailchimp');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Privy or Mailchimp for cart recovery?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Privy when signup forms and their follow-up offers are the work you want to consolidate. If Mailchimp already holds your campaign audience, first demonstrate recovery with the intended Shopify audience before paying for migration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Privy capture while Mailchimp sends?",
    "answer": "Treat this as an integration design to validate, not an automatic handoff. Demonstrate the exact destination audience, offer identifier, permission state and unsubscribe behavior before enabling messages."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should happen before connecting Shopify?",
    "answer": "Mailchimp advises pausing existing welcome automation flow maps or classic automations associated with the audience before connection. Inventory other active senders too, then test the replacement before activation."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do discounts affect margin?",
    "answer": "In a hypothetical $742 basket with 61% product gross margin, product cost remains $289.38. A 20% discount reduces revenue to $593.60 and product gross profit to $304.22, before other costs. The discount costs $148.40, not $90.52."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should we migrate an existing Mailchimp audience?",
    "answer": "Move only after reconciling subscribed and suppressed contacts, segment definitions, forms and active messages. A new capture tool alone is not evidence that the existing email program needs replacing."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does attributed recovery prove a better tool?",
    "answer": "No. First check correct audience entry and message cancellation, then use an adequately powered randomized comparison to estimate incremental profit. No merchant experiment or product performance result is reported here."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Decide whether to bring capture and follow-up into Privy or preserve a working Mailchimp audience while connecting Shopify recovery. The cost of rebuilding that audience matters alongside the signup experience.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Capture-led evaluation', tool: a.name, reason: 'Demonstrate the signup offer and the messages that follow it.' }, { label: 'Existing audience evaluation', tool: b.name, reason: 'Demonstrate Shopify recovery in the audience your team already operates.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the audience you actually have</h2>
        <p>A store with a functioning Mailchimp newsletter, established segments and approved templates faces a different decision from a store starting email marketing. Moving the first store requires rebuilding operating knowledge as well as installing an app. For the second, demonstrating a signup offer and its follow-up in one workspace may be the more useful evaluation.</p>
        <p>Privy's published scope includes popups, email, SMS and visual automation. Mailchimp documents a Shopify connection to a selected audience and synchronization of customer and order data. These overlapping capabilities do not justify labeling either product universally basic or declaring a revenue winner.</p>
        <p>Primary sources checked November 24, 2026: <a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy's product overview</a>, <a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp's connection guide</a> and <a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/mailchimp-shopify-syncing/">Mailchimp's syncing guide</a>. Published scope does not establish the behavior of your account or plan.</p>
        <h2>Three configurations worth distinguishing</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Practical benefit to evaluate</th><th>Work it creates</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Privy captures and sends</td><td>Keep the form promise and follow-up editable together.</td><td>Rebuild retained Mailchimp segments, templates and exclusions if migrating.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mailchimp sends with Shopify connected</td><td>Use the audience and campaign process already maintained by the team.</td><td>Confirm the selected audience, synchronized data and recovery entry conditions.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Privy captures while Mailchimp sends</td><td>Change the capture experience while retaining the email program.</td><td>Validate the supported connection and maintain offer, permission and suppression mappings across tools.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Proposed audience and offer acceptance cases</h2>
        <p>These are demonstrations to run in your own controlled setup, not tests we observed. For a new signup, record the exact form promise, address, destination audience and eligibility state. Trace the welcome message back to that promise. A cart reminder must not silently replace a promised offer with a conflicting code.</p>
        <p>Next use an existing Mailchimp subscriber who submits the Privy form again. Check whether the intended segment and offer update without starting duplicate welcomes. Then use a suppressed contact: record how the proposed integration handles the submission and ensure historical suppression is not overwritten by a routine data sync. Resolve any legitimate new subscription through the intended permission process.</p>
        <p>Finally, create a controlled cart and complete its purchase after a reminder is queued. Inspect the sender's actual cancellation behavior and repeat the same cart event to check replay handling. If capture, purchase and suppression state arrive on different paths, keep an event trace for each path rather than assuming the two products coordinate automatically.</p>
        <h2>Connect without waking the wrong welcome flow</h2>
        <p>Mailchimp's connection guide advises pausing existing welcome automation flow maps or classic automations associated with the audience before connecting Shopify. That warning matters when an established list meets a newly connected store. Document every welcome trigger and who owns it before connection; distinguish the initial customer sync from a new signup that should receive a welcome.</p>
        <p>After a controlled sync, reconcile a sample of existing subscribers, unsubscribed contacts and new signups against the selected audience. Use Mailchimp's documented integration activity logs to investigate sync activity. A contact appearing in the audience is not sufficient proof of permission or recovery eligibility. Keep live sending paused until the intended cases behave correctly.</p>
        <h2>Compare retained costs with replacement costs</h2>
        <p>Request current quotes for the same usable contact population and intended features. Include retained Mailchimp charges if it remains the sender, Privy capture or sending charges, any connector, template rebuilding, staff training and time investigating mapping failures. Do not compare an entry price for one configuration with a full sending setup for the other. This page does not verify a fixed price or rating.</p>
        <p>For a hypothetical $742 basket at 61% product gross margin, product cost is $289.38 and full-price product gross profit is $452.62. A 20% discount removes $148.40 from revenue; revenue becomes $593.60 and product gross profit becomes $304.22. Shipping, payment fees, returns and messaging costs are still excluded. Recovered revenue alone can therefore hide a costly incentive.</p>
        <h2>Choose a reversible cutover</h2>
        <p>If Mailchimp already works, first investigate whether the identified problem is the form experience, the Shopify connection or the recovery workflow. Replacing all three at once makes diagnosis harder. If moving sending to Privy, export the required permission and suppression records, rebuild essential segments and compare membership before activation. Disable the old owner of each message and reconcile queued sends before its replacement goes live.</p>
        <p>Keep a rollback record of audience mappings, disabled automations and unresolved contacts. Do not reactivate both recovery senders to compensate for missing data. A separate tool such as Sequenzy belongs in the evaluation only if it addresses a documented unmet requirement and its Shopify data, sending ownership and suppression handling are demonstrated; adding a lifecycle label does not solve those interfaces.</p>
        <p>Select the configuration that preserves audience intent with manageable operating work. Validate delivery and cancellation first; separately evaluate incremental product gross profit with a suitable randomized comparison. This guide offers decision criteria and proposed checks, not a completed merchant trial.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Mailchimp FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
